# =============================================================================
# STIG checklist adjudications -- consumed by `it-ckl`.
#
# This is the part of the checklist that does NOT change per machine. Every
# deviation, compensating control and POA&M entry we have already argued out
# lives here once, keyed by STIG id, instead of being retyped into STIG Viewer
# on every box. `it-ckl` merges these over the SCAP results.
#
# Rendered per profile, so an entry can say something different on an AI node
# than on the EMI laptop -- which matters, because several of these deviations
# only apply to one of them.
#
# Keys may be the STIG id (UBTU-24-200640), the V-ID (V-270645), or the SSG
# rule name. Fields:
#   status           not_a_finding | open | not_applicable | not_reviewed
#   finding_details  what an assessor reads as the evidence
#   comments         internal note
#   override: true   accept this status even when SCAP reports a FAILURE.
#                    Without it a SCAP failure always wins, so a stale entry
#                    here can never quietly mark a broken control compliant.
#
# Anything NOT listed here stays Not_Reviewed, which is the honest default.
# =============================================================================

# ---- Banner text: DCSA rather than the DoD Standard Mandatory Notice --------
# Three rules check for the exact DoD string. We show the site banner by
# choice, so these are permanent, accepted deviations rather than gaps.
UBTU-24-200640:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    The system displays the {{ 'DCSA' if not (emi_unclass | default(false) | bool) else 'organizational unclassified' }} warning banner
    required by the site's ISSM, not the DoD Standard Mandatory Notice.
    Banner text is enforced by configuration management (usg_remediate role,
    /etc/issue, /etc/issue.net and the sshd Banner directive) and is identical
    across the fleet. The control objective -- a consent banner shown before
    logon -- is met; only the literal text differs.
  comments: Approved text deviation. See docs/compliance.md, "Approved deviations".

# The GRAPHICAL logon banner text -- same deviation as the console/SSH banner
# above. compliance.md has said "three banner-text rules" since the first scan
# and this one was never actually written down, so it showed as an open finding
# with no justification attached.
UBTU-24-200660:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    The graphical logon displays the site warning banner required by the
    ISSM, not the DoD Standard Mandatory Notice. The banner is enabled and
    LOCKED via dconf by configuration management (desktop_hardening), so a
    user cannot suppress or alter it. The control objective -- a consent
    banner shown before granting local graphical access -- is met; only the
    literal text differs.
  evidence_cmd: "gsettings get org.gnome.login-screen banner-message-enable 2>/dev/null; gsettings get org.gnome.login-screen banner-message-text 2>/dev/null | head -c 200"
  comments: Approved text deviation, same basis as UBTU-24-200640 / 200680.

UBTU-24-200680:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    /etc/profile.d/ssh_confirm.sh displays the site warning banner and requires
    explicit acknowledgement before an interactive SSH session proceeds. The
    consent gate is present; the text is the site banner rather than the DoD
    Standard Mandatory Notice.
  comments: Approved text deviation.

# ---- USB mass storage ------------------------------------------------------
# Gated on usb_storage_enabled, NOT on the profile. This entry used to render
# only for EMI, so dev-13 -- a development workstation with exactly the same
# deliberate deviation -- reported UBTU-24-300039 Open with "NO SITE
# ADJUDICATION RECORDED" against it. Every box that re-enables the module has
# the same justification and now says so; the AI nodes leave the module
# blacklisted (usb_storage_enabled is false there) and simply pass the rule,
# so nothing is rendered for them.
{% if usb_storage_enabled | default(true) | bool %}
UBTU-24-300039:
  status: open
  finding_details: |
    The usb-storage kernel module is deliberately NOT disabled on this system.
    desktop_hardening strips the `install usb-storage /bin/false` and
    `blacklist usb-storage` lines that USG writes, and loads the module.
{% if is_emi | default(false) | bool %}
    Justification: this system performs authorized data transfer to and from
    removable media as its mission function, which the control would prevent
    entirely.
{% else %}
    Justification: this is an engineering workstation whose users move
    deliverables on approved removable media. Disabling the module removes the
    capability for every user rather than restricting it to those authorized,
    and would defeat the group-based mount restriction below -- with the module
    blacklisted no block device ever appears, so there is nothing left to
    authorize.
{% endif %}
    Compensating controls, all enforced by configuration management:
      1. USBGuard enforces a device authorization allow-list. Devices not on it
         are blocked at USB enumeration, before any filesystem is seen, and the
         decision is written to the audit trail.
      2. Mount rights are restricted to members of the '{{ usb_access_group | default('dta') }}' group
         via a udisks2 polkit rule and a udev rule; a user outside that group
         cannot mount removable media even once the device is authorized.
      3. Device authorization decisions and mount events are audited.
    Net effect: removable media is restricted to approved devices operated by
    named, authorized users, which is the control objective. The prescribed
    means of meeting it -- removing the capability from the kernel -- is what
    this system cannot use.
  evidence_cmd: "grep -rn 'usb.storage' /etc/modprobe.d/ 2>/dev/null || echo 'usb-storage not blacklisted (expected)'; echo '-- usbguard:'; systemctl is-active usbguard 2>/dev/null; usbguard list-rules 2>/dev/null | head -10; echo '-- {{ usb_access_group | default('dta') }} group:'; getent group {{ usb_access_group | default('dta') }} || echo 'group absent'"
  comments: >-
    POA&M -- accepted deviation with compensating controls. Review the evidence:
    USBGuard must be active with a populated policy and the mount group must
    exist, or the compensating controls are not actually in place and this is a
    plain finding.
{% endif %}

# ---- Shared library group ownership ----------------------------------------
# The scanner and the STIG disagree about scope here, and the STIG wins.
#   DISA check : find /lib /lib64 /usr/lib /usr/lib64 -type f -name '*.so*' ! -group root
#   SSG OVAL   : the same trees, but EVERY file, group must be root
# The rule's own title is "group-owned by root OR A SYSTEM ACCOUNT", so the
# SSG check is stricter than the requirement it implements. Ubuntu ships setgid
# helpers under /usr/lib whose group is load-bearing -- utempter needs group
# utmp to write the utmp database, dbus-daemon-launch-helper needs group
# messagebus for system-bus activation -- and chgrp root breaks both. Those
# groups are system accounts, which the rule permits.
#
# usg_remediate applies the DISA fix verbatim (usg_fix_library_group), so any
# shared object that drifts off group root is corrected on every pull. The
# evidence below prints the DISA check first: if it returns nothing, the
# requirement is met. The second half lists whatever SSG is still objecting to,
# with each group's GID, so an assessor can confirm for themselves that the
# remaining entries really are system accounts (GID < 1000) and not, say, a
# library group-owned by a user.
UBTU-24-300009:
  status: not_a_finding
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    Shared library files under /lib, /lib64, /usr/lib and /usr/lib64 are group
    owned by root. This is enforced on every configuration-management run
    (usg_remediate applies the DISA remediation for this rule), so the DISA
    check returns no output -- see the evidence below.
    The automated SSG rule root_permissions_syslibrary_files reports a failure
    because it checks every file in those directories rather than the shared
    library files the STIG names, and requires group root rather than "root or
    a system account". The files it objects to are Ubuntu's setgid helper
    binaries, whose group membership is what makes them work and which the
    rule's own text allows. On a stock Ubuntu 24.04 install there are exactly
    two, both owned by system accounts:
      /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/utempter/utempter   group utmp (gid 43)
      /usr/lib/dbus-1.0/dbus-daemon-launch-helper   group messagebus (gid 101)
    utempter is setgid utmp so terminal emulators can update the utmp login
    database without being setuid root; dbus-daemon-launch-helper is setgid
    messagebus so the system bus can activate services. Changing either to
    group root does not harden anything -- it breaks the mechanism that keeps
    those binaries from needing root at all.
  evidence_cmd: "echo '== DISA check (any output is a finding):'; find /lib /lib64 /usr/lib /usr/lib64 -xdev -type f -name '*.so*' ! -group root -exec stat -c '%n %G' {} + 2>/dev/null || true; echo '== remaining non-root groups in SSG scope (name group gid; gid<1000 = system account):'; find /lib /lib64 /usr/lib /usr/lib64 -xdev -type f ! -group root -printf '%p %g\\n' 2>/dev/null | while read -r f g; do echo \"$f $g $(getent group \"$g\" | cut -d: -f3)\"; done | head -40"
  comments: >-
    Scanner scope exceeds the STIG's. Read the evidence: the DISA check must be
    empty, and every group in the second list must be a system account. If
    either is untrue this is a real finding and must be remediated, not accepted.

# ---- Time synchronization --------------------------------------------------
UBTU-24-600160:
  status: not_a_finding
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    chrony is configured with an authoritative site time server
    ({{ stig_time_servers | default(['the site NTP server']) | join(', ') }}) in
    /etc/chrony/chrony.conf using `server` directives with maxpoll set per the
    benchmark. The benchmark's rendered value names a public .mil NTP pool,
    which this system cannot reach and must not depend on; the site server is
    the authoritative source for this enclave.
  comments: Site time source. Closeable in the scan by tailoring var_multiple_time_servers.

# ---- ufw rate limiting -----------------------------------------------------
UBTU-24-600200:
  status: not_a_finding
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    ufw rate-limits inbound SSH (`ufw limit`). Remaining listening ports are
    application service ports whose normal traffic pattern exceeds ufw's
    six-connections-in-thirty-seconds threshold; rate-limiting them would deny
    legitimate service. This rule has no automated check content, so it is
    reported as a failure by the scanner regardless of configuration.
  comments: Manual/OCIL rule -- always reports fail in SCAP. Assessed by inspection.

# ---- Smart card / CAC ------------------------------------------------------
UBTU-24-300020:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    This system uses local accounts with password authentication only. It is
    not joined to a directory service and there is no CAC/PIV reader in the
    approved hardware configuration, so smart-card authentication controls do
    not apply. The rules are de-selected in the USG tailoring file.
  comments: No directory service, no CAC reader. Approved.

# ---- /var/log file modes ---------------------------------------------------
# Enforced, but state this rather than leave it bare: the rule failed fleet-wide
# for a while even though the fix was correct, because apt and dpkg recreate
# their logs 0644 AFTER the early remediation pass and before the scan. It is
# now swept again at the end of the role and by a daily timer. NO override --
# if the scanner still reports a failure this stays Open with the evidence
# attached, which is what an assessor needs to see.
UBTU-24-700010:
  status: not_a_finding
  finding_details: |
    No file under /var/log carries any of the 0137 permission bits. The mode is
    enforced by configuration management (usg_remediate) at build time and
    re-applied daily by the stig-log-perms.timer systemd unit, because apt,
    dpkg and unattended-upgrades recreate their log files world-readable and
    would otherwise reopen this finding between builds.
    btmp, wtmp and lastlog are excluded by the STIG check itself.
  evidence_cmd: "echo '== DISA check (any output is a finding):'; find /var/log -xdev -perm /137 ! -name '*[bw]tmp' ! -name '*lastlog' -type f -exec stat -c '%n %a' {} + 2>/dev/null || true; echo '== enforcement timer:'; systemctl is-enabled stig-log-perms.timer 2>/dev/null; systemctl list-timers stig-log-perms.timer --no-pager 2>/dev/null | head -3"
  comments: >-
    Deliberately NOT overridden. If SCAP still reports a failure the evidence
    above names the offending files -- treat it as a real finding.

# ---- GRUB / single-user-mode password ---------------------------------------
{% if 'CHANGEME' in (grub_password_pbkdf2 | default('CHANGEME')) %}
# Rendered ONLY while grub_password_pbkdf2 is still the CHANGEME sentinel. Set
# a real hash and the grub_password role runs, the rule passes, and this entry
# disappears -- it can never linger as a stale excuse for a fixed control.
UBTU-24-102000:
  status: open
  finding_details: |
    No GRUB superuser password is configured: grub_password_pbkdf2 is still the
    placeholder value, so the grub_password role deliberately takes no action
    rather than write a password nobody holds. Without it, anyone with physical
    access can edit the kernel command line at the boot menu and obtain a root
    shell.
    This is materially worse on this build than the generic case. The root
    volume is LUKS-encrypted with the key sealed to the TPM against PCR 7 only,
    which measures Secure Boot policy and NOT the kernel command line -- so the
    volume unseals and decrypts regardless of what is appended at the boot menu.
    Physical access therefore reaches decrypted data.
    Interim mitigation: physical access to these systems is controlled by the
    facility's access procedures.
    Remediation, which requires no rebuild and takes about a minute per box:
      sudo it-grub set        # this box, interactive, applies immediately
      sudo it-grub status     # confirm
    Fleet-wide, generate the hash once and vault it into group_vars as
    grub_password_pbkdf2:
      sudo it-grub hash
  evidence_cmd: "it-grub status 2>/dev/null || echo 'it-grub not present'"
  comments: >-
    POA&M with a named, immediate remediation -- not an accepted deviation.
    Close it by running `sudo it-grub set` on the box, or by vaulting the
    PBKDF2 hash for the fleet. This entry stops rendering once the hash is set.
{% endif %}

# ---- Audit log offload -----------------------------------------------------
UBTU-24-900240:
  status: not_a_finding
  finding_details: |
    /etc/cron.weekly/audit-offload runs weekly and stages a compressed copy of
    the rotated audit logs to {{ usg_audit_offload_dir | default('/opt/ia/audit-offload') }},
    retaining the most recent {{ usg_audit_offload_keep | default(8) }} sets.
    This system is standalone with no reachable log collector; the staged
    archives are collected to approved removable media by the ISSO on the
    documented schedule.
  comments: Standalone offload to staged media.

# ---- Encryption at rest ----------------------------------------------------
{% if luks_expected | default(true) | bool %}
UBTU-24-300004:
  status: not_a_finding
  finding_details: |
    The system uses LUKS2 full-disk encryption on the root volume, configured
    at install time. Verify with: cryptsetup status /dev/mapper/<root> and
    lsblk -f (FSTYPE crypto_LUKS on the underlying partition).
  comments: Verified per box during build; see the inventory report.
{% endif %}

# =============================================================================
# THE MANUAL / OCIL RULES
#
# These carry no automated check, so SCAP leaves them Not_Reviewed and an
# assessor expects a human's evidence. Most of that evidence is one command's
# output, so `evidence_cmd` runs it and embeds the real result in the checklist
# -- what the box actually reports, not a claim in prose. Read the output before
# accepting the status: these are pre-filled with the answer that is correct for
# a standard build, and a box that has drifted will show it here.
# =============================================================================

# Emergency / temporary accounts (V-270682)
UBTU-24-200250:
  status: not_applicable
  finding_details: |
    No emergency or temporary accounts are provisioned on this system. All
    accounts are permanent, created by the local_accounts role from the
    version-controlled account list, and reviewed as part of the build. The
    control applies to accounts created for a limited period, of which there
    are none.
  evidence_cmd: "awk -F: '$3>=1000 && $3<65534 {print $1\" uid=\"$3}' /etc/passwd; echo '-- expiring accounts:'; for u in $(awk -F: '$3>=1000 && $3<65534 {print $1}' /etc/passwd); do e=$(chage -l $u 2>/dev/null | grep -i 'account expires'); echo \"$u: $e\"; done"
  comments: Confirm the list matches the approved account baseline.

# Restrict functions / ports / protocols / services (V-270719)
UBTU-24-300041:
  status: not_a_finding
  finding_details: |
    ufw is enabled with a default-deny inbound policy. The only inbound rules
    are the documented service ports required by this profile, applied by the
    firewall role from version control; SSH is additionally rate-limited. No
    unauthorized listening service is permitted, and the listening set is
    recorded in the box's inventory report.
  evidence_cmd: "ufw status verbose; echo; echo '-- listening:'; ss -H -tulpn | awk '{print $1, $5, $7}'"
  comments: Compare the listening set against the approved services list.

# DoD PKI-established CAs (V-270745)
UBTU-24-600060:
  status: not_applicable
  finding_details: |
    This system performs no PKI-based authentication and terminates no TLS for
    DoD users: it is standalone, uses local accounts with password
    authentication, and is not joined to a directory service. There is no
    trust path for which DoD PKI CAs would be the required anchors. Should
    PKI-based authentication be introduced, this rule becomes applicable and
    must be re-assessed.
  comments: Tied to the same de-scoping as the smart card / CAC rules.

# Only necessary members in the sudo group (V-270748)
UBTU-24-600130:
  status: not_a_finding
  finding_details: |
    Membership of the sudo group is defined in version control (the
    local_accounts role) and is limited to named administrator accounts. No
    service or application account holds sudo, and NOPASSWD is not permitted --
    sudo_remove_nopasswd and sudo_require_authentication both pass in the
    automated scan.
  evidence_cmd: "getent group sudo; echo; echo '-- sudoers.d:'; ls -l /etc/sudoers.d/; echo; grep -rn NOPASSWD /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/ 2>/dev/null || echo 'no NOPASSWD entries'"
  comments: Review each member against the approved administrator list.

# Audit storage capacity (V-270816)
UBTU-24-900920:
  status: not_a_finding
  finding_details: |
    /var/log/audit is sized to retain the required audit record volume, with
    auditd configured to alert and act before the filesystem fills
    (space_left_action and admin_space_left_action are set, and the
    corresponding automated rules pass). Rotated logs are staged off weekly by
    /etc/cron.weekly/audit-offload, which bounds on-disk growth.
  evidence_cmd: "df -h /var/log/audit /var/log 2>/dev/null; echo; grep -E '^(max_log_file|num_logs|space_left|admin_space_left|space_left_action|admin_space_left_action|disk_full_action)' /etc/audit/auditd.conf"
  comments: Confirm the partition size meets the org's retention requirement.

# Cryptographic protection of audit tool integrity (V-270831)
UBTU-24-909890:
  status: not_a_finding
  finding_details: |
    AIDE is installed and its database includes the audit tools, so any change
    to them is detected by cryptographic hash. The related automated rules
    (package_aide_installed, aide_build_database, aide_check_audit_tools) all
    pass, and AIDE runs on a schedule with notification enabled.
  evidence_cmd: "grep -E '/usr/sbin/(auditctl|auditd|ausearch|aureport|autrace|augenrules)' /etc/aide/aide.conf /etc/aide/aide.conf.d/* 2>/dev/null | head -20 || echo 'no explicit audit-tool entries found'"
  comments: Evidence must show the audit tools covered by an AIDE rule.

# ---- Smart card / PKI family (V-270663, V-270721, V-270736) ----------------
# usg_harden de-selects these in the tailoring file, so a TAILORED scan does not
# raise them. An untailored scan does, and an assessor reading the checklist
# still needs the reason, so carry the adjudication explicitly.
UBTU-24-100660:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    SSSD is installed but not enabled: this system is standalone with local
    accounts and password authentication, is joined to no directory service,
    and has no CAC/PIV reader in its approved hardware configuration. There is
    no multifactor authentication service for SSSD to provide. De-selected in
    the USG tailoring file.
  comments: Same de-scoping as the smart card rules. Re-assess if a reader is added.

UBTU-24-400020:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    Smart card logins are not implemented: no CAC/PIV reader is present in the
    approved hardware configuration and the system uses local password
    authentication only. pam_pkcs11 is deliberately removed from the auth stack
    -- with no reader attached it produced a "no smart card found" failure on
    every authentication.
  comments: Approved. Re-assess if CAC is introduced.

UBTU-24-400370:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    No PKI-based authentication is performed on this system, so there is no
    authenticated certificate identity to map to a local account. Applies only
    once smart card / PKI authentication is in use.
  comments: Dependent on the smart card rules above.

# ---- Session lock image (V-274871) -----------------------------------------
UBTU-24-200043:
  status: not_applicable
  override: true
  finding_details: |
    The session lock conceals all previously visible information. The rule's
    automated check requires the screensaver picture-uri to be EMPTY; this
    system instead displays the organizational lock-screen image, applied and
    LOCKED by configuration management (desktop_branding), which conceals the
    display just as completely. The control objective is met; only the
    prescribed means of meeting it differs.
  evidence_cmd: "gsettings get org.gnome.desktop.screensaver picture-uri 2>/dev/null; grep -rn 'picture-uri\\|lock-enabled\\|idle-delay' /etc/dconf/db/*.d/ 2>/dev/null | head -10"
  comments: Deliberate site deviation -- the org wallpaper is a mandated control of its own.
